							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3311.msg37368#msg37368">Quote from: yuchunyang on September 21, 2007, 07:15:27 pm</a></div><div class="quote">I think it&#039;s funny that you use that picture, even though you hold against that kid for not using his head or using logic. If the Black Panthers used their heads, they wouldn&#039;t exist because Fred Hampton would know he would get a bullet in his head by the CIA. Yeah, the smart thing to do would not to resist because it&#039;s very obvious what is going to happen if you resist. <br /><br />I would agree with you if the power dynamic between this guy and this police was equal. The guy did no action but is held responsible for no action,yet the police did all the action and are not held responsible. <br /><br />I don&#039;t think that he was just resisting because he was surprised or because that&#039;s a natural reaction to getting grabbed (and it is a natural reaction to be surprised and react when you&#039;re grabbed but not when you&#039;re pretty fully aware that you&#039;re surrounded by cops). For the most part he reacted that way because he was scared shitless, this guy isn&#039;t down and out and has nothing left to lose, this guy is a white university student who is trying to look tight by trying to make a celebrity look bad. When the cops got hold of him he got scared and thought his mommy or daddy or some kind of similar figure to save him, in the same way that he has been rescued his whole life. <br /><br />I mean I ain&#039;t crying over that this guy got tazered, lots of more fucked up shit has happened, in the united states, all over the world, but its dangerous to think about it in that way,in a sort of ride the fence way.<br /><br />Not trying to diss or offend.<br /><br /><br /><br /></div><br />nah man, good points no offense taken.&nbsp; i&#039;ve thought about the irony in that a bit my self since posting this stuff.&nbsp; <br /><br />i guess i&#039;d clarify a bit by saying what i&#039;ve said on a few other boards.&nbsp; that i don&#039;t necessarily think this guy was wrong to resist an unjustfied police action, in an abstract philosophical sense at least.&nbsp; my point was that in this instance his resistance was pointless and stupid.&nbsp; &nbsp;<br /><br />my point in posting the picture was that all the general public can letimately do about this kind of incident is organize into a body that can bring to bear practical and effective resistance of a type that has the potential to really force change in society.&nbsp; to form organizations which can resist police brutallity in a manner that doesn&#039;t only result in a more severe beat down for the resister.&nbsp; <br /><br />my point is that resistance without a strategy guiding it is not only generally ineffective but usually counter productive, and is therefore ultimately not justified.&nbsp; even when what is being resisted is also not justified.&nbsp; the consequences of an action must be taken into concideration and if more damage will come as a result than benefit it should not be undertaken.<br /><br />i don&#039;t agree with the police&#039;s actions here, and i don&#039;t really fault the kid for what you point out was probably a fear response.&nbsp; my point is that this specific act of resistance was unwise and unjustified because it resulted only in greater hardship for the resister with no strategic benefit for him or his cause.<br /><br />so i think you&#039;re pretty much right, except that i disagree that the black panthers didn&#039;t use their head.&nbsp; they used their heads very much imo to come up with a way to prevent individual victims of police brutallity from having to fend for themselves as this college student had to.&nbsp; my point is this kid&#039;s energy (and likely clean criminal record) could have been put to better use organizing as the panthers did to effect real resistance, than they were trying to pointlessly resist this specific arrest.</div>
